F. SLOWER STEPPER PULSE, STILL RESPONSIVE
- Find the existing blue connector pulse. Keep its look, color, route, labels, spacing, and active treatment. Do not redesign the stepper.
- Set the pulse to about 450–550 ms total. A nonadjacent jump (1 → 5) is ONE continuous pulse, with no per-stage delay.
- Navigation and the active state update IMMEDIATELY. Only the visual pulse animates. Never activate, mount, or execute intermediate stages. Forward and backward directions must both be correct.
- If the user clicks again mid-animation, cancel/replace the old animation and navigate to the latest target immediately. When using the Web Animations API, handle the cancelled animation's rejected promise so there is no console error and no stuck state.
- Use one CSS/Web Animations indicator, no per-frame React state, and no heavy blur. Keep it inside the connector geometry and avoid mobile overflow.
- Keep the existing page-content fade (about 180–240 ms, 4–6 px). No white flash and no remounting of the shell, globe, or graph for animation.
- prefers-reduced-motion: use an immediate change or a very short fade.
Acceptance: the pulse is visibly slower but still quick. Rapid repeated clicks always land on the latest stage.

E. HOMEPAGE PRISM: SUBTLE DEPTH AND A REPEATING STATUS CYCLE
E1. Depth.
- Keep the prism at its current balanced size. Do not enlarge it. Preserve the geometry, facets, blue edges, core, existing scan/orbit motion, and proportions. Add no meshes, rings, panels, or new platform.
- Add restrained depth with CSS and card styling only: (a) one cool blue radial light behind the prism, peak alpha no more than about 0.18; (b) a soft contact/grounding shadow beneath the prism, built as a CSS ellipse under the canvas or by enhancing an existing in-scene shadow; (c) a gentle card highlight/shadow that follows the rounded corners. At most two low-opacity blue/cyan gradients.
- No neon aura, bloom, dark vignette, or heavy drop shadow. Keep the canvas transparent so there is no rectangular seam. Record the old and new values.

E2. Repeating status strip.
- Determine whether any real fingerprint computation is connected to this card (search the code for a digest bound to the card's data). Report what you find.
- One sequence owner only: no duplicate timers or canvases. Cycle: "Scanning SHA-256 fingerprint…" with changing hex characters for about 2.5–3.5 s, then a completed state for about 1.5–2 s, then back to scanning with a short cross-fade. The strip has fixed reserved height, with the two states stacked and cross-faded, so the card never shifts.
- The completed-state wording must be truthful. If NO real check backs it (the expected case), use "Fingerprint ready", never "verified". Use "Fingerprint verified" only if you found and can name the real computation. Do not display a fixed seeded hash as if it authenticated a user's file. With no real digest, cycle neutral hex characters.
- Update at most 8–12 times per second, or write to a text ref. Do not call setState per frame. Clear all timers on unmount (StrictMode-safe). Start when the scene is loaded and visible, and pause when offscreen (IntersectionObserver) or when the tab is hidden (visibilitychange).
- Accessibility: the scrambling hex text is aria-hidden. Do not use a live region that re-announces every cycle. Provide one static visually-hidden label.
- prefers-reduced-motion: show a stable static line ("Fingerprint ready"), with no scramble and no loop.
- Keep lazy loading and the WebGL fallback. The fallback shows the same compact strip and no old UI.
Acceptance: subtle depth, a normal-sized unchanged crystal, a status that keeps cycling without layout shift, and no implied verification that did not happen.

B. A REAL, COHERENT NEW-CASE WORKFLOW
The user creates a case once, and the same data appears throughout the application.

B1. Inspect the data architecture first and report it.
- Find: the active-case selector, the New Case action, the case/evidence/timeline/entity/relationship/transaction models, the store or context, case switching, dashboard selectors, graph data, the existing Add Timeline Step and Add Evidence File dialogs, and the report/export handlers.
- Establish ONE source of truth per case. Derive every stage's view from the selected case. Remove or bridge any duplicate arrays that would drift out of sync. In particular, the existing Add Timeline Step / Add Evidence File dialogs must write to this same source of truth for the active case.
- Keep seeded cases intact and keep their current edit behavior. Do not alter or persist-over their seed data. The repository owns user-created cases unless the existing architecture already unifies both, in which case reuse it.
- If a working Java/API backend is already wired, use its existing contract and verify real responses. Never invent endpoints or bypass authentication. Otherwise implement a client-side repository.

B2. Repository and persistence.
- Use the project's existing persistence first. Otherwise use IndexedDB (structured records and file blobs). No new package. Add a schema version and a simple migration hook.
- Feature-detect storage. The Preview may run in a sandboxed iframe where IndexedDB/localStorage throw. If storage is unavailable, fall back to in-memory state and show a short neutral notice such as "Changes are kept until you close this tab". Do not claim the data persists unless a reload test in Preview actually passed.
- Scope stored cases to the current signed-in user/role per the existing auth/RBAC. Do not expose one user's cases to another. Respect existing role permissions for creating, editing, and deleting. Clear or lock user-scoped data on sign-out following existing conventions.
- Handle quota and write failures: keep the form contents, show an actionable message, never discard input silently.
- Do not log case content to the console. Do not claim "server synced", a backend connection, remote processing, OCR, AI analysis, or integrity checks unless that operation really ran.

B3. New Case flow.
Use the existing New Case entry point. Build a polished, glass-styled, viewport-safe multi-step dialog (portal, bounded height, internal scroll, above the sticky header), working on desktop and mobile.
- Only the case title is required. The case ID is auto-generated if blank. Everything else is optional. The user can add details later through the workspace's existing add dialogs. The wizard must not feel like a form wall.
- Steps: (1) Case details: title, ID, category, priority, status, incident date/time + timezone, investigator, affected person/organization, summary. (2) People and entities: label, type (person, phone, email, bank account, wallet, domain/URL, IP/server, organization, country, other supported types), role, identifiers, country if explicitly entered, notes. (3) Financial movements: reference ID if given, date/time, from/to entity, amount, currency, type/status, evidence link. (4) Timeline events: title, description, date/time/timezone if known, entities involved, optional amount, evidence link, warning flag with reason. (5) Evidence: ID, filename/title, type, source/acquisition notes, size, date/time, optional file upload where the existing UI supports it. (6) Relationships: from-entity, to-entity, type, direction, optional evidence link.
- Back/Next/Cancel/Create Case, required-field markers, inline errors, and preserved values between steps. Confirm before discarding a form that has entered data.
- Integrity rules: the case ID is unique case-insensitively (generate a collision-safe ID, for example with a short random suffix, if blank or taken). Trim strings and normalize blank optionals to absent. Relationships, transactions, and events reference entities and evidence by stable internal IDs, never by display name. Renaming an entity must not break references. Reject self-relationships. Deleting an entity or evidence record requires confirmation that lists its dependents, then cleans up references safely.
- Amounts: parse as decimals and store as integer minor units (or decimal strings). Never use floating-point sums. Validate the currency against ISO 4217-style codes. Never assume INR or any currency. Totals are per currency.
- Dates: store the instant (UTC ISO) plus the entered timezone. Sort by instant. Undated items go at the end, labeled "Date not recorded". Future dates produce a warning, not a block.
- Identifier formats (phone, email, IP) produce soft warnings, not blocks.
- Evidence file hashes: if file bytes are present, compute SHA-256 locally with Web Crypto (feature-detect it; it is available only in secure contexts). Set a size cap of about 50 MB, since digest() is not incremental; above that cap, or for metadata-only entries, show "Not recorded" with the reason. Tie any async hash computation to its case and record ID, so a result that finishes after a case switch can never be written into the wrong case.
- On success: save, set the new case active, close the dialog, navigate to the stage the app normally opens first, and show a brief "Case created" confirmation.
- Edits update the same record. No shadow copies. Deletes confirm first.

B4. Propagation. Everything derives from the selected case:
- Case selector and header: ID, title, status, priority, investigator.
- Overview/KPIs: evidence, event, entity, and warning counts, plus known exposure per currency, and any other existing metric. Never show another case's numbers (for example the old "14 entities").
- Globe: plot only countries that are explicitly entered and that exist in the current country-coordinate data. Entered countries without coordinates are listed but not plotted. Draw arcs only for relationships or transactions that connect entities in different plotted countries. If nothing is plottable, show a neutral empty state ("No locations recorded for this case") and keep the globe functional. Do not change the globe's input or scroll policy.
- Evidence view: the case's records and any entered or uploaded metadata.
- Analyze: only clues the user entered or functionality that actually ran. Do not fabricate OCR output.
- Timeline: chronological by instant, undated last.
- Warnings: from user-entered flags plus a short, explicit list of deterministic rules, each with a plain-language reason. Example rules: a transaction references a missing entity; evidence has no recorded hash; a future-dated event; an evidence date earlier than the incident date. Do not manufacture anomalies.
- Reset all per-case UI state when the case changes: selected node and path, hover, filters, open inspector, per-stage scroll, open dialogs, and in-progress exports.
- Show a concise status or loading state only where real work occurs. No fake spinners.

B5. Calculations.
- Compute counts, totals, intervals, and metrics from records and recompute on every change. Distinguish "no data recorded" from a real zero.
- Exposure uses actual transaction records, per currency. Deduplicate only on a stable transaction/reference ID. Otherwise list the records and flag "possible duplicates unresolved".
- Intervals use available timestamps, timezone-aware. Do not invent missing times.
- If risk/priority scoring already exists, keep its rules. If a score is needed, use a small transparent rule on recorded facts and show the top contributing reasons. Never present it as forensic certainty.

C. CONNECTION GRAPH FROM THE ACTIVE CASE
- Keep the current graph engine, layout, interactions, and PageRank/Dijkstra. Do not change the algorithms except to handle new data safely.
- Nodes come from the case's entities. Edges come only from explicit relationships and transaction records. When a transaction is entered, the edge carries from/to, amount, currency, timestamp, reference ID, and evidence link. Multiple transactions between the same two entities are aggregated into one edge (count and per-currency totals in its tooltip/inspector) unless the current renderer already draws parallel edges. Preserve edge direction.
- Node and edge IDs are the stable record IDs. Merge two entities only on a reliable matching identifier. Otherwise keep them separate.
- Handle edge cases without NaN, Infinity, or crashes: zero nodes, one node, no edges, disconnected components, and unreachable paths in the route tracer. Repopulate the tracer's two dropdowns from the current graph and clear any stale selection.
- On case create or switch, remount or reset the graph state (selection, hover, path, zoom), fit the new graph, and keep default node sizes, with no auto-zoom into a random node.
- Keep hover highlighting of the node and its connected edges, and keep click selection persistent after the pointer leaves.
- Few or no relationships: show the real nodes and a readable empty state. Create no fake connections.
Acceptance: add two related entities and one transaction, and the graph shows them. Switch cases, and no stale node or edge remains. Remove a relationship, and exactly that edge disappears.

D. REPORT AND EXPORTS FOLLOW THE ACTIVE CASE
- Extend the existing report model (one shared builder, for example buildReportModel). Do not create a second one. Every existing export format (PDF, JSON, CSV, others) must be generated from it, with no new formats.
- Reports reflect the SAVED state of the active case. Unsaved wizard or edit-form drafts do not appear. Switching cases changes the output.
- Content: overview, plain-language summary, timeline, evidence register, entities and relationships, per-currency financial summary, recorded warnings, integrity results with exact scope, and next review points, where data exists. Use "Not recorded" for absent fields and never insert data from another case, OCR output, fictional hashes, or "verified" claims for unchecked files.
- Include a data-origin line in report metadata and in the JSON, worded neutrally with no visible "demo"/"sample" labels: "Case origin: Pre-loaded case" or "Case origin: Entered in this workspace". Describe integrity results only as far as computation actually ran.
- PDF: a valid, readable, multi-section PDF (begins with %PDF), not a screenshot, not renamed text, not a placeholder. Reuse the existing PDF utility from the previous export work. Add at most one PDF package only if none exists, and no table or font packages. Wrap long filenames, IDs, URLs, and hashes; use a monospace font for hashes; paginate cleanly. For non-Latin text the font cannot render, use a documented substitution and flag it. Never emit garbled glyphs.
- JSON: parses, is structured, and is complete for the selected case. CSV: RFC 4180 escaping, a UTF-8 BOM, and formula-injection neutralization that does not alter real numbers.
- Generate on demand with the correct MIME type and a sanitized filename. Show a brief loading state, disable the button while generating, and revoke object URLs after a short delay (about 1–4 s).
- Test a title-only case and confirm the export does not crash and shows "Not recorded".
- If Preview blocks the download, verify the generated Blob programmatically (header bytes, size, parse) and report the download as "Not verified".
Acceptance: create or edit a case, export PDF and JSON, and the files match its values. Switch cases, and the output changes. No export reverts to placeholder content.

